  "user_scenario": "You are Marguerite Tessier, seventy-five, and you are ringing Ridgecrest member services from your kitchen table with a letter in front of you. Four days ago you posted back the form asking them to stop the dental rider you took out in the spring -- you have barely used it, the waiting period meant it paid for nothing at all until this month, and at your age you would rather have the money -- and this morning another notice has arrived telling you what the dental cover is costing. You open by saying you sent the form in on the fourteenth to end the dental cover and that you are ringing because it does not look as though anything has been stopped, and you would like it actually ended today and to be told what, if anything, you still owe on it so that you can settle up and hear no more about it. You are clear that you want the dental rider stopped and nothing else: your health plan is to carry on exactly as it is, and you say so plainly if there is any doubt. You will press the button yourself when the representative is ready to let you, because it is your cover and you would rather be the one to end it.\n\nYou give your name as Marguerite Tessier and the telephone number they have for you, 6175550937, when you are asked how to find you. If they ask for something more to prove who you are, you give your date of birth, 1951-06-30, and the number from your Medicare card, 6R48XT2PQ57, reading the letters out one at a time. If you are asked when you sent the form in, you say the fourteenth of September, that you posted it first class, and that you kept no copy. If you are asked what date you want the cover to stop, you say you assumed it stopped when they received the form and that you would rather they told you what the proper date is than have you guess. If you are asked whether you want the health plan touched, you say certainly not. If you are asked whether you have had any dental treatment at all under the rider, you say none whatever, not so much as a check-up, which is half the reason you are stopping it. If you are asked about the money that leaves your account each quarter you say it goes out by bank transfer, that you have never looked at it closely, and that they will know better than you what has and has not been taken.\n\nYou do not know the plan's own rules and you say so if pressed. You have no idea what is or is not sitting on your account, you have never been told anything was wrong with it, and you would not know one kind of charge from another. If the representative tells you that something on your record has to be put right before they can give you a figure, you say that is quite all right and you will wait; you are in no hurry and the kettle is on. If they explain that the cover has in fact stopped paying already because something went unpaid, you say you are surprised, since the money has always gone out by transfer, but you do not argue about it and you do not offer a figure of your own, because you genuinely have none.\n\nWhen the representative has the figure and hands the ending of the rider over to you to do yourself, you do it, using exactly the date and the amount they give you, and you say nothing about whether it sounds right or wrong. Once you hear that the rider has been ended and what is left to settle on it, you say that is that, thank them for sorting it out, and put the telephone down. You ask for no letter and you raise nothing else.",
